\section{Measure blocks}{

A measure is declared in a comment block that opens with \verb{/**} at the start
of a line and holds an \verb{@measure} tag. Its code is everything after the
block, up to the next measure block or the end of the file. Code before the
first measure block, such as macro definitions, runs before every measure
in the file.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode sas">}}\preformatted{/**
 * Mean height for one sex
 *
 * Averages SASHELP.CLASS heights.
 *
 * @measure heights
 * @param sex `enum[F, M]` Which sex.
 * @param [min_age=11] `integer` Youngest age included.
 * @return One row with the mean height.
 * @output WORK.RESULT
 * @provenance https://example.com/heights.sas
 */
proc means data=sashelp.class(where=(sex = "&sex" and age >= &min_age));
  var height; output out=work.result mean=height;
run;
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The first paragraph is the title, and any further paragraphs and the
\verb{@return} text describe the measure. \verb{@measure} can name the measure;
otherwise it is named after the file.

Each \verb{@param} declares an argument the model supplies. Brackets mark an
optional argument and can give a default (\verb{[year=2025]}). A type code can
follow: \code{string} (the default), \code{integer}, \code{number}, \code{boolean},
\code{enum[value, ...]}, or any of these followed by \verb{[]} for an array.

\verb{@output} names the dataset the measure creates, which is returned as a data
frame: \code{LIBREF.TABLE}, or \code{TABLE} in \code{WORK}. The default is \code{WORK.RESULT}.
\verb{@output none} returns the listing instead. \verb{@provenance} links to where
the code came from, and can be repeated.
}

\section{How a measure runs}{

Each argument is set as a global macro variable with \verb{CALL SYMPUTX} before
the code runs. Every declared argument is set on every call, and an unset
optional one is empty, so a value from an earlier call never leaks into a
later one. Strings and enums are the value itself, booleans are \code{1} or \code{0},
and arrays are their elements separated by spaces, with strings
single-quoted, ready for \verb{where x in (&name)}. Refer to a string argument
as \verb{\%superq(name)} to keep SAS from resolving macro triggers in it.

The output dataset is deleted before the code runs. A log line starting with
\code{ERROR} fails the measure, and so does a run that leaves no output dataset.
}

